am requiring government ID verification for 'reduced friction' access. Simon notes the announcement is difficult to follow and doesn't mention Anthropic, but still requires a Google Form application for the best tools.\",\n    95→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    96→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/14/openai-cyber/\",\n    98→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-14\",\n    99→    \"topics\": [\"openai\", \"cybersecurity\", \"ai-security\", \"anthropic\", \"gpt-5-4\"],\n   100→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   101→  },\n   102→  {\n   103→    \"title\": \"Cybersecurity Looks Like Proof of Work Now\",\n   104→    \"description\": \"The UK's AI Safety Institute published an evaluation of Claude Mythos's cyber capabilities. Drew Breunig noted that the more tokens (money) spent, the better the security results. This creates an economic equation: 'to harden a system you need to spend more tokens discovering exploits than attackers will spend exploiting them.' This makes open source libraries MORE valuable since tokens spent securing them are shared across all users, countering the vibe-coding replacement narrative.\",\n   105→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (citing Drew Breunig, AISI)\",\n   106→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   107→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/14/cybersecurity-proof-of-work/\",\n   108→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-14\",\n   109→    \"topics\": [\"cybersecurity\", \"ai-security\", \"economics\", \"open-source\", \"vibe-coding\"],\n   110→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   111→  },\n   112→  {\n   113→    \"title\": \"Andrej Karpathy on the growing gap in AI capability understanding\",\n   114→    \"description\": \"Simon shared Andrej Karpathy's insight about the growing gap in understanding AI capabilities based on access points: OpenAI's free 'Advanced Voice Mode' runs on an older GPT-4o era model (knowledge cutoff April 2024) and 'will fumble the dumbest questions,' while their highest-tier paid Codex model can 'go off for 1 hour to coherently restructure an entire code base.' Karpathy attributes this to (1) verifiable reward functions for coding vs. writing, and (2) higher B2B value driving more team focus.\",\n   115→    \"author\": \"Andrej Karpathy (via Simon Willison)\",\n   116→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   117→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/10/karpathy-ai-capability-gap/\",\n   118→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-10\",\n   119→    \"topics\": [\"andrej-karpathy\", \"openai\", \"ai-capabilities\", \"coding\", \"llms\"],\n   120→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   121→  },\n   122→  {\n   123→    \"title\": \"State of AI in 2026: LLMs, Coding, Scaling Laws (Lex Fridman Podcast #490)\",\n   124→    \"description\": \"Lex Fridman podcast with Nathan Lambert and Sebastian Raschka discussing the state of AI in 2026. Topics covered include LLMs, AI coding and programming, dev tooling, scaling laws, technical breakthroughs, closed vs. open LLMs, AI agents, GPU technology, AGI, and China's role in AI development. 4.5-hour comprehensive interview.\",\n   125→    \"author\": \"Lex Fridman, Nathan Lambert, Sebastian Raschka\",\n   126→    \"source\": \"Podcast\",\n   127→    \"url\": \"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EV7WhVT270Q\",\n   128→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-01\",\n   129→    \"topics\": [\"lex-fridman\", \"llms\", \"scaling-laws\", \"ai-coding\", \"agents\", \"gpu\", \"china\", \"agi\"],\n   130→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n   131→  },\n   132→  {\n   133→    \"title\": \"Parasail raises $32M to feed 'tokenmaxxing' AI developers\",\n   134→    \"description\": \"Parasail, a cloud computing service for AI model inference, raised $32M Series A. The company generates 500 billion tokens daily and rents processing time at 40 data centers in 15 countries. The investment signals a fractured future where developers use hybrid architectures: open models for initial screening to drive down costs, then frontier models for final answers. Andreas Stuhlmüller (Elicit CEO) noted they've moved to open models because sending 100,000s of requests to API endpoints is 'pretty rough.' Samir Kumar (Touring Capital) expects inference to be at least 20% of software development costs.\",\n   135→    \"author\": \"Tim Fernholz\",\n   136→    \"source\": \"TechMedia\",\n   137→    \"url\": \"https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/15/parasail-raises-32m-to-feed-tokenmaxxing-ai-developers/\",\n   138→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-15\",\n   139→    \"topics\": [\"inference\", \"cloud-compute\", \"tokens\", \"open-models\", \"hybrid-architecture\", \"venture-capital\"],\n   140→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   141→  },\n   142→  {\n   143→    \"title\": \"Claude Opus 4.7 announced by Anthropic\",\n   144→    \"description\": \"Anthropic released Claude Opus 4.7 on April 16, 2026. Key improvements: 3x better resolution on Rakuten-SWE-Bench production tasks vs Opus 4.6; SWE-bench Verified: 87.6%, SWE-bench Pro: 64.3%; first Claude model with high-resolution image support (2576px / 3.75MP); better at code quality, test quality, financial analysis, and data visualization. Available in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Amazon Bedrock, Cursor, and GitHub. Same pricing ($5/$25) but new tokenizer increases real cost by up to 35%.\",\n   145→    \"author\": \"Anthropic\",\n   146→    \"source\": \"TechMedia\",\n   147→    \"url\": \"https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-opus-4-7\",\n   148→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-16\",\n   149→    \"topics\": [\"anthropic\", \"claude\", \"llm-release\", \"coding\", \"benchmarks\", \"image-support\"],\n   150→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   151→  },\n   152→  {\n   153→    \"title\": \"The AI code wars are heating up\",\n   154→    \"description\": \"The Verge examines the intensifying competition between OpenAI, Google, and Anthropic to build the best AI coding tools, even as developers express fears about AI coding tools. The article questions which model actually writes the best code and r